<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Llíria</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Llíria is a town in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, about 25 kilometres north-west of the city of Valencia. Above it rises the hill of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edeta">Tossal de Sant Miquel</a>, site of Edeta, the capital of the Iberian Edetani, and the town continued as the Roman municipium of Edeta, whose baths and sanctuary have been excavated. It is now known for its musical tradition.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lliria.jpg" alt="Llíria" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pelayo2 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:VGeneral_Liria.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Beneath the modern town lie the ruins of one of the most important Iberian cities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. Then known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edeta">Edeta</a>, it was the administrative centre of Edetania, an extensive territory between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jucar">Júcar</a> and the Palancia River. The city stood on the hilltop called Sant Miquel, which overlooks the present town. After Roman troops destroyed it in 76 BCE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-sertorius">Quintus Sertorius</a> moved the city downhill to its current location.</p><p>Under the Romans Llíria was as important as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a> or Sagunt. The town is rich in Roman finds, among them a large leisure complex with a temple, shops, pools and hot baths. Excavations have also uncovered one of the largest buried caches of coins found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, popularly called the Treasure of Carrer Duc de Llíria, which totals some 6,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> denarii minted in the first and third centuries. A mosaic of the Twelve Labours of Hercules came from a Roman house at Can Porcar and is displayed at the National Archaeological Museum of Spain in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>. The town's own archaeological museum holds imagery from the mosaic's original setting and other Roman objects.</p><p>The first church in Llíria was built in 1238 by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-i-of-aragon">James I of Aragon</a>, after his victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moors">Moors</a> and the conquest of the Valencian region. The Church of the Blood stands on the site of a mosque and is a typical example of Valencian Gothic architecture; some remains of the mosque can still be seen. In 1919 it was gazetted as a National Monument, the first religious monument in the Valencian Community to receive that distinction.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Llíria is the capital of the Camp de Túria area in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>, at an altitude of 164 metres. Its traditional economy is based on agriculture, with textiles, construction materials, plastics and furniture also important. It is the end of the Metrovalencia train system, and about 24,000 people lived there in 2022. Both Spanish and Valencian are spoken.</p><p>The town is called the musical capital of its region, and on 30 October 2019 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> declared it a Creative City in the category of Music. Several thousand residents play instruments, and the town is known for two rival wind bands. The Banda Primitiva was formed by the Franciscan friar Antoni Albarracín Enguídanos in 1819, and the second band split from it in 1903 to form the Unió Musical. Its Holy Week processions, which carry lifelike sculptures of scenes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>'s arrest to his burial, date from medieval times, and the town also keeps the feast days of Saint Vincent and Saint Michael.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Llíria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Each tribe or confederation had its centres: Basti (Baza) for the Bastetani, the largest confederation, Edeta (<b>Llíria</b>) for the Edetani, Iltrida (Lleida) for the Ilergetes, Kese for the Cessetani, Laieta for the Laietani.</blockquote><a href="/culture/iberians">Iberians · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>Their capital, Edeta, stood on the hill of Tossal de Sant Miquel above <b>Llíria</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/edetani">Edetani</a></li></ul></section>
